course: biology
lesson: 163
title: Madagascar: lemurs of every kind and the fossa
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Madagascar is a big island off the coast of Africa that broke away from other land so long ago that its animals went their own way, and nearly all of them live nowhere else.
explain: Lemurs are primates, cousins of monkeys and apes, and with no monkeys on the island to compete they spread into about a hundred kinds, from one smaller than a hamster to the singing indri.
explain: Picture a cold morning when you stand by a sunny window to warm up: ring-tailed lemurs do just that, sitting with arms spread wide like little sun worshippers.
explain: In the cartoon Madagascar a lemur king throws parties and a fossa pack is a gang of villains; the real lemurs are led by females, and the real fossa is a lone hunter related to mongooses.
fact: Ring-tailed lemurs sit facing the sun with arms spread to warm up after cold nights.
fact: Male ring-tailed lemurs have stink fights, wiping scent onto their tails and waving them at rivals.
fact: In ring-tailed lemurs and many other lemurs, females lead the group and get the best food, which is unusual among mammals.
fact: Sifakas are leaping lemurs that hop sideways on two legs across open ground and can leap about ten metres from tree to tree.
fact: The aye-aye taps wood with a long thin middle finger and listens for hollow tunnels with grubs inside, doing the job of a woodpecker, because Madagascar has no woodpeckers.
fact: Madame Berthe's mouse lemur is the smallest primate in the world, about thirty grams, and fat-tailed dwarf lemurs store fat in their tails to sleep through the dry season.
fact: The fossa is Madagascar's biggest meat-eater, shaped like a slim cat but related to mongooses, and lemurs give special alarm calls for it.
fact: Lemurs live wild only on Madagascar and a few nearby islands; about a hundred kinds are known, and many are in danger.
fact: Until about one or two thousand years ago Madagascar had giant lemurs as big as gorillas, and elephant birds, the heaviest birds ever, with eggs bigger than a rugby ball.
fact: The indri is the biggest living lemur and sings loud songs in family groups; scientists found rhythms in indri songs a little like music.
fact: The blue-eyed black lemur is one of the few primates other than humans with blue eyes, and it lives only in a small area of northwest Madagascar.
fact: Golden bamboo lemurs eat young bamboo that contains cyanide, a poison; their bodies cope with a poison that would harm other animals, and eating a food no one else can eat means less competition.
exercise: Why do ring-tailed lemurs sit with their arms spread in the morning? | To warm up in the sun after a cold night | To dry their fur in the sun after a long swim in the river | To show they are hungry | To call the rain
exercise: What happens in a ring-tailed lemur stink fight? | Males wipe scent on their tails and wave them at rivals | Females throw rotten fruit at each other until one of them gives up | Babies roll in mud | Lemurs spray water from their noses
exercise: Who usually leads a group of ring-tailed lemurs? | A female | The oldest male | The smallest baby | A fossa
exercise: How does a sifaka cross open ground? | It hops sideways on two legs | It crawls on its belly | It rolls like a ball | It swings from vine to vine
exercise: About how far can a sifaka leap between trees? | About ten metres | About ten centimetres | About a hundred metres | About one kilometre
exercise: Why does an aye-aye tap on a branch? | To listen for hollow tunnels with grubs inside | To call its mother | To scare away birds | To crack open hard nuts by tapping them against a branch
exercise: Which job does the aye-aye do in Madagascar that birds do elsewhere? | The woodpecker's job of finding grubs in wood | The owl's job of hunting mice | The eagle's job of catching fish | The parrot's job of cracking open hard seeds with a strong beak
exercise: How heavy is the smallest primate, Madame Berthe's mouse lemur? | About thirty grams | About three kilograms | About thirty kilograms | About three hundred grams
exercise: Where does a fat-tailed dwarf lemur store fat for the dry season? | In its tail | In a hump on its back | In its cheeks | Under its chin
exercise: What is the fossa related to? | Mongooses | Lions | Dogs | Bears
exercise: How do lemurs react when a fossa comes near? | They give special alarm calls | They run up to it and try to play with it, like puppies with a dog | They fall asleep | They change colour
exercise: Where do lemurs live in the wild? | Only on Madagascar and a few nearby islands | All over Africa | In the rainforests of South America and in the jungles of India | In Indian jungles
exercise: How big were Madagascar's giant lemurs long ago? | As big as gorillas | As big as elephants | As big as mice | As big as horses
exercise: What were elephant birds? | The heaviest birds ever, with eggs bigger than a rugby ball | Birds that carried elephants | Tiny birds that rode on the backs of elephants to pick off their fleas | Birds with trunks
exercise: What is special about the indri? | It is the biggest living lemur and sings loud songs | It is the smallest lemur and never makes a sound | It lives under water | It has a striped tail
exercise: What is unusual about the blue-eyed black lemur? | It is one of the few primates besides humans with blue eyes | It can see in total darkness and even through the walls of its nest | It has three eyes | Its eyes glow blue at night
exercise: Why can the golden bamboo lemur eat bamboo full of cyanide? | Its body copes with a poison that would harm others | The cyanide is all washed out of the bamboo by the rain before it eats it | It only pretends to eat it | Bamboo has no poison at all
exercise: Why is eating a poisonous food useful for the golden bamboo lemur? | Few other animals compete for it | It makes its fur thick and shiny and golden all over | It makes it venomous | It helps it fly
test: «King Julien's the boss so male lemurs rule, right» What is right? | No, in ring-tailed lemurs the females lead | Yes, the biggest and strongest male is always the king of the troop | Yes, lemurs crown a king every year | No, the fossa is the boss of lemurs
test: In the cartoon Madagascar, fossas hunt in big gangs. What is real? | A real fossa is a slim mongoose relative that usually hunts alone | Fossas are a kind of wolf that live in packs and hunt lemurs together | Fossas only eat grass | Fossas live in the sea
test: «4 ring-tails sunbathing, how many striped tails is that, duh» | 4 | 8 | 16 | 1
test: «3 lemurs, no wait 5, how many ears altogether» | 10 | 6 | 15 | 20
test: «my mate reckons lemurs are just monkeys» What is right? | They are primates, but lemurs are a separate branch, not monkeys | Yes, every lemur is a monkey | No, lemurs are a kind of cat | No, lemurs are rodents, like squirrels and rats, that live in trees
test: What would happen to the aye-aye if Madagascar suddenly had lots of woodpeckers? | It would have to share its grubs with them | It would learn to fly | Nothing at all could change | It would start eating the woodpeckers' eggs instead of grubs
test: A museum shows an egg much bigger than a rugby ball from Madagascar. Whose egg is it? | An elephant bird's | A fossa's | A giant lemur's | An aye-aye's
test: «why do lemurs scream when a fossa comes, they're just loud lol» What is right? | They give special alarm calls to warn each other | They are singing to it to make it go back to sleep | They want it to play | They are hungry
test: A lemur fits in the palm of your hand and weighs as much as a few coins. Which is it? | A mouse lemur | An indri | A sifaka | A ring-tailed lemur
test: Why could lemurs spread into about a hundred kinds on Madagascar? | The island was cut off for a very long time and had no monkeys to compete | They came by boat a few years ago | Every lemur is really the same kind of animal, just in a different colour of fur | Zoos bred them there
test: «who sings in the forest at dawn, sounds like a siren» in Madagascar. Which animal fits best? | An indri family | A fossa | A mouse lemur | A chameleon
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the aye-aye taps upon the wood of trees. | The detection by sound of hollow tunnels containing larvae | The communication of alarm to other aye-ayes by means of a loud tapping | The cracking of the shells of nuts | The deterrence of approaching birds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the taxonomic affinities of the fossa. | It is related to the mongooses notwithstanding its feline appearance | It is a true member of the cat family, closely related to the puma | It is a species of wild dog | It is a species of lemur
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the social organisation of the ring-tailed lemur? | Females occupy the position of leadership within the group | A single male is selected as king of the group | No individual exercises any leadership | Leadership is assumed by the youngest animals
exam: Ascertain the significance of the capacity of the golden bamboo lemur to consume bamboo containing cyanide. | Access to a food resource for which there exists little competition | The acquisition of venom for the purposes of defence | The colouring of its fur to a golden hue that hides it in the bamboo forests | The ability to dispense with water entirely
